<p class="hint disclaim">For planning only. Results depend on what you enter and on your policy, and they aren’t a quote, an appraisal or legal advice. Default figures are illustrative.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout" style="padding-top:44px"><article class="prose">
<h2>How the checkup is weighted</h2>
<p>Coverage, itemized estimates and evidence count most, because they decide whether appraisal is the right tool and how well your number holds up. Paperwork items count less because they are easier to fix.</p>
<h2>What your score suggests</h2>
<ul><li><strong>75 or higher:</strong> a solid file. A professional look at the dollar gap is the next step.</li>
<li><strong>45 to 74:</strong> close the open items first — each one strengthens your position.</li>
<li><strong>Under 45:</strong> demanding appraisal now could lock in a low number. Build the file first.</li></ul>

<h2>Questions about this estimator</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is my score saved?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. Answers stay in your browser and disappear when you leave the page.</p></div></details><details><summary>Should I share the result with an appraiser?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. A screenshot of your to-do list is a useful starting point for a first conversation.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does a high score mean I will win?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. It measures preparation, not outcome. No appraiser can guarantee an award.</p></div></details></div>
